WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the protests have a time been violent  but it didn't deter  hundreds from turning out for a sixth  week  on wednesday rallies were sparsely  attended yet clashes started early  as a group of indigenous misak tried to  topple a statue of christopher columbus  they were stopped by riot police with  tear gas and sun grenades  dozens were injured  we are performing an ancestral  revitalization of the historical memory  of the indigenous  populations of colombia in the midst of  the 21st century there should be no  space for racist representations of  colonial power  that marginalizes and excludes our  communities  human rights watch says it has  documented at least  20 killings as well as illegal beatings  sexual assaults and arbitrary detentions  it accuses the colombian police of  egregious abuses  and is calling on the government to take  urgent measures to protect people's  right  and commit to police reform  we are dealing with a police force that  lacks basic standards of training and  accountability  and that above all doesn't have the  sufficient oversight to act in a manner  that ensures the basic respect of human  rights  president ivan duke says he will propose  changes to policing that include putting  body cameras on officers  and creating a new human rights  directorate  the protesters are demanding a  demilitarization of the police force  and that officers crimes are placed  under the jurisdiction of civilian  courts  international pressure to do so is  growing  demonstrators have also been staging a  protest in front of the hotel tecandama  where the leaders of the protests have  been meeting with the delegation of the  inter-american commission on human  rights  tasked on investigating the abuses  committed during the protests  the government is not listening and  watchdog institutions in the country are  controlled by the government  criminal complaints fall on deaf ears  that's why we need international  organizations to help us  to achieve the safety guarantees we  cannot get internally  members of the commission have also  spoken to government officials  judges and civil society organizations  during a three-day visit  they are expected to release  recommendations next week  in the meantime negotiations between  protesters and the government  remained stalled leaving the crisis on  the streets  with no end in sight alice al-jazeera  bogota

